'''Nyulnyul''' is an [[Language death|extinct]] [[Australian Aboriginal language]], formerly spoken by the [[Nyulnyul people]] of [[Western Australia]].

[[Carmel Charles|Mary Carmel Charles]] is documented as the last fluent speaker of the Nyulnyul language of [[Western Australia]].<ref name = book>{{cite book |last = Charles |first = Mary  |translator = William B. McGregor | title = Winin – Why the Emu Cannot Fly | publisher = Magabala Books|year=2000 |orig-year = 1993  | location = Broome, WA|isbn = 1-875641-07-6}}</ref>

==Phonology==
=== Consonants ===
Nyulnyul has seventeen consonant phonemes, with five distinct places of articulation. Nyulnyul is a morphologically complex language with both prefixing and suffixing.<ref name="Pacific Linguistics">{{cite book |last1=McGregor |first1=William B. |title=The Nyulnyul language of Dampier land, Western Australia. Volume 1: Grammar |date=2011 |publisher=Pacific Linguistics |location=Canberra |isbn=9780858836471 |page=65 |url=https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/handle/1885/205021 |access-date=10 October 2022}}</ref>

